\begin_layout Section
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Four mathematicians walk into a bar,
 and decide to play a game of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fastest growing function
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 To be more precise,
 consider the preordered set of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\N\to\R$
\end_inset

,
 where we say 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
dominates
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $f\geq g$
\end_inset

 for all but finitely many inputs.
 The four mathematicians each write down a short description of a function in a napkin,
 and their goal is that their secret function dominates the functions chosen by the others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The analyst tries to cram as many exponents as she can into her napkin,
 winding up with something that looks like 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=\e^{\e^{\e^{\e^{x}}}}$
\end_inset

.
 The combinatorialist names the well-known 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{TREE}(n)$
\end_inset

 function.
 The computability theorist names the so-called busy beaver function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{BB}(n)$
\end_inset

,
 obliterating the competition.
 The set theorist gets disqualified because her function provably dominates any function that can be described in less than 1000 characters,
 including itself,
 thereby causing a paradox.
 This is not going to be a set theory talk.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I'll be talking about the computability theory of functions that grow very fast – faster,
 in fact,
 than any computable function.
 This does not cause a paradox because these functions are not,
 in and of themselves,
 computable.
 We will be seeing some proofs that I think are cute.
 This talk is intended for a general audience.
 No prior knowledge of computability theorem is assumed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Computability Theory Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Review notion of programming language/Turing machine.
 Idealized model,
 because we're mathematicians.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Unsolvability of Halting Problem:
 There is no computer program 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 which takes as input the code for another program,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

,
 and outputs the answer to the question:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Will 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 finish executing in a finite amount of time?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Proof:
 Suppose there were such a program 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

.
 We shall write a program 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

,
 designed to cause a contradiction.
 The program 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# File p.py:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

def h(x):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	(code for H)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

myself = open(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

p.py
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).read()
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

if h(myself):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	while True:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

		pass
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout point
If we were being truly rigorous,
 we'd need to write a quine here but whatever.
 Kleene figured out how to do it in 1938.
 Standard treatments do something slightly different to get around that,
 using the artificial definition:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Will 
\begin_inset Formula $P(P)$
\end_inset

 finish executing in a finite amount of time?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 I don't like that.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Anyway,
 a quick check shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $H(P)$
\end_inset

 will be incorrect,
 causing a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Nevertheless,
 the function 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 is a valid function 
\begin_inset Formula $\N\to2$
\end_inset

 that you can define.
 It's just not write-able as a computer program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Probably worth pointing out that I wrote 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

,
 but I could just as well have written like,
 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{<\omega}$
\end_inset

 or something.
 In computability theory we usually go between perspectives freely,
 but abstractly we work with 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

 (or strings,
 if convenient) because we have to fix 
\emph on
some
\emph default
 basic universe of discussion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Maybe sketch that we identify programs with numbers:
 To go between perspectives,
 identify a program with its binary representation in the computer,
 which in turn can be seen as a natural number (you need to add a one at the top to disambiguate leading zeros).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Definition:
 A function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\colon\N\to\N$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
computable
\emph default
 if there is a computer program 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 which accepts one argument as input,
 and for every
\shape italic
 
\emph on
input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 it
\shape default
\emph default
 outputs in finite time the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Remark:
 Not every computer program corresponds to a valid function,
 because it may fail to terminate on some inputs.
 For this reason,
 computability theorists often work with 
\emph on
partial functions
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\colon\N\rightharpoonup\N$
\end_inset

 instead.
 But this will not be relevant for us.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
We can imagine,
 as a thought experiment,
 a computer which is given access to a black-box that computes 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

.
 And then,
 we have access to new programs,
 and hence new functions (e.g.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 itself).
 The new resulting class of functions is called the class of 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable functions
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Generally,
 for every function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\colon\N\to\N$
\end_inset

,
 computable or not,
 we can imagine a computer program that has access to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 as a black box.
 This induces a pre-order on functions called 
\emph on
Turing Reducibility
\emph default
:
 We say 
\begin_inset Formula $f\geq_{\mathrm{T}}g$
\end_inset

 if there is a computer program with access to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 as a black box that calculates 
\begin_inset Formula $g(x)$
\end_inset

 on input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 We say 
\begin_inset Formula $f\equiv_{\mathrm{T}}g$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $f\geq g$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g\geq f$
\end_inset

 – this means that they have the same computational content.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Important note:
 When these black boxes are queried,
 the only information that is conveyed is the output,
 and not,
 for example,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

how long did the black box take to reply
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
I distinguish between 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computer programs that accept one input
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 vs.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computer programs that will be executed on their own
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The former I will represent using capital letters 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

,
 etc.
 and the latter by 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 and maybe 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
An equivalence class of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\N\to\N$
\end_inset

 is called a 
\emph on
Turing degree
\emph default
.
 The set of Turing degrees forms a partially ordered set.
 Do the drawing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The whole Halting Problem business can be generalized.
 If you have an oracle 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 you can define the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Halting Problem relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
 Given a program 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{f}$
\end_inset

,
 does it halt in finite time?
 And by a similar reasoning as before,
 the resulting function 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{f}$
\end_inset

 is not computable in 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Exercise:
 
\begin_inset Formula $f\geq_{\mathrm{T}}g\implies H^{f}\geq_{\mathrm{T}}H^{g}$
\end_inset

.
 (Have the audience members think over this,
 and try to get them to produce a proof.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Sketch:
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 is calculated by a program 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 which uses 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at some points.
 Then,
 to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{g}(P)$
\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 uses 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

),
 write a new program 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{P}=f\mathrm{ify}(P)$
\end_inset

 which does the same thing,
 but every instance of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(x)$
\end_inset

 is replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $G(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\mathrm{ify}$
\end_inset

 is itself computable.
 Thus,
 the new program 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{P}$
\end_inset

 uses 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 not 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 but it otherwise has exactly the same behavior.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{g}(P)=H^{f}(f\mathrm{ify}(P))$
\end_inset

,
 and the latter could be written as a computer program that refers to 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{f}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Corollary:
 
\begin_inset Formula $f\equiv g\implies H^{f}=H^{g}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Halting Problem
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is a well-defined function on Turing degrees.
 Do a drawing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Busy Beaver
\end_layout

\begin_layout In Preamble
\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\BB}{\mathop{\mathrm{BB}}}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(n)$
\end_inset

,
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

busy beaver function
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as follows:
 Take all valid computer programs that can be written in at most 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 characters or bytes or whatever.
 There are finitely many.
 Of those programs,
 some will finish executing in a finite amount of time,
 while others will run forever.
 We set 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(n)$
\end_inset

 to be the maximal among the finite runtimes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Unlike real computers,
 our model of computation is assumed to run in a predictable amount of steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
This function was defined by Radó (not Rado) in 1962.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Think over with audience:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB\colon\N\to\N$
\end_inset

 is Turing equivalent to the Halting Problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Sketch:
 HP computes BB b/c,
 with access to HP,
 we can iterate over all programs with at most 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 characters,
 collect just the ones that halt,
 execute all of those,
 and take the maximal runtime.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Hidden detail:
 This algorithm requires writing an entire computer simulator,
 to run these programs and tally how long they take to run.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
BB computes HP:
 To tell if a program 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 halts,
 run it for 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(\mathrm{size}(P))$
\end_inset

 time.
 If it hasn't halted by this time,
 it will never halt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Again,
 we need to write an entire computer simulator.
 And we need it to have the ability 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

run this computer program for 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 amount of time,
 and tell me if it has terminated by then
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
In fact,
 from this proof we recover a corollary:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Corollary:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f\colon\N\to\N$
\end_inset

 dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 computes the Halting Problem,
 and in particular is not computable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Corollary:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB$
\end_inset

 is not dominated by any computable function.
 But could it possibly be incomparable to one?
 Maybe there could be a computable function that repeatedly catches up to it then loses ground.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB$
\end_inset

 dominates any computable function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Proof:
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a computable function,
 associated to the program 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
 Define a computable sequence of programs 
\begin_inset Formula $P(0)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(1)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(2)$
\end_inset

,
 etc.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

with the following form.
 Here is 
\begin_inset Formula $P(73)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

t = F(73)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

while t > 0:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	t = t - 1
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Then,
 we have the following properties:
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
\begin_inset Formula $P(n)$
\end_inset

 can be written in 
\begin_inset Formula $\log_{10}(n)+19+c$
\end_inset

 characters,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is however many characters it takes to implement 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
\begin_inset Formula $P(n)$
\end_inset

 runs for at least 
\begin_inset Formula $f(n)$
\end_inset

 steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Note also that,
 for all but finitely many values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq\log_{10}(n)+19+c$
\end_inset

.
 For all such values,
 we then have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\BB(n)\geq\mathrm{runtime}(P(n))\geq f(n),
\]

\end_inset

which concludes the proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
I guess it's important here that your programming language lets you write a number 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 in logarithmically many characters.
 I hope no one's favorite programming language fails to meet that bar,
 though it's technically doable if you're trying to be really crummy about it?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
One thing you'll note is that the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB$
\end_inset

 depends on your choice of programming language and model of computation.
 Without a lot of added context,
 it's not possible to flat-out say,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

oh,
 I calculated 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(3)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 because your 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(3)$
\end_inset

 can be different from mine.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The version introduced by Radó in 1962 is currently being studied by a project called the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bbchallenge
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The version they study is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The input 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is the number of states in the machine,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The output is the number of steps until termination,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The tape is assumed bidirectional,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
and there are some other conventions to ensure that everyone is talking about the same thing.
 For example,
 every transition moves the head left or right – there are no standing still transitions.
\end_layout

