\begin_layout Point
It is also known that there's a 745-state Turing machine 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 that halts iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 is inconsistent.
 As such we conclude:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 (Assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 is consistent) For large enough values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 (in this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq745$
\end_inset

),
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 cannot prove any upper bound of the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(n)$
\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 does not determine the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Proof sketch:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 was able to prove 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB(745)$
\end_inset

 is less than,
 say,
 a billion,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 would be able to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 never halts by proving that it hasn't halted by time 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

one billion
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{ZFC}$
\end_inset

 would prove its own consistency,
 which is impossible by Gödel's second incompleteness theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Martin's Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
We saw that the Busy Beaver dominates all computable functions,
 and any function that dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $\BB$
\end_inset

 must also compute the Halting Problem.
 This indicates that there is some amount of computational power that stems from 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

growing very fast
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Of course,
 not all computational power is derived in this manner.
 For example,
 the Halting function 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 does not even go to infinity – all its computational power stems from the specific pattern of ones and zeros.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
One may wonder:
 What kind of computational power can we harness from dominating all computable functions?
 A theorem by Martin (1966) characterizes this computational power:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Definition:
 A degree 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

high
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 if it satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $X'\geq0''$
\end_inset

.
 (Do the drawing.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 If a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 dominates all (total) computable functions,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is high.
 Conversely,
 every high degree computes such a function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Note the word 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

total
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 here.
 As I (might have) mentioned before,
 not every computer program induces a total function,
 and it's not easy to tell what programs do or do not do so.
 Thus,
 it's often convenient to work with partial functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\N\rightharpoonup\N$
\end_inset

 instead.
 One might wonder if that makes much difference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
(Get audience to define 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dominate a partial function
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 If a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 dominates all partial computable functions,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f\geq_{\mathrm{T}}0'$
\end_inset

.
 Conversely,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 and every degree above it can compute such a function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Proof:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 dominates all partial computable functions,
 then in particular it dominates the function 
\begin_inset Formula $n\mapsto$
\end_inset

 runtime of the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-th program.
 Say 
\begin_inset Formula $f+c$
\end_inset

 majorizes it.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 computes the HP by the algorithm:
 To see if 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 halts,
 run it for 
\begin_inset Formula $f(P)+c$
\end_inset

 time.
 Output whether it's halted by now or not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Conversely,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 computes such a function as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

def f(n):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	out = 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	for all programs P of size at most n:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

		if H(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

P(n)
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

			out = max(out,
 P(n))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	return out
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Clearly,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is well-defined for all values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 and will dominate any program 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq\mathrm{length}(P)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Final Curiosities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Computing a function that dominates every computable function is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computational strength
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 property.
 Sometimes these are also called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

highness properties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 In this case,
 dominating all total computable functions is a highness property that coincides with the computational property that's also called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

high
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 and dominating all 
\emph on
partial
\emph default
 computable functions coincides with the property of being above 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
We also like to consider 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lowness properties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 – that is,
 properties of an oracle that make it weak at computation.
 One such property is the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Definition:
 A degree 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
hyperimmune-free
\emph default
,
 or 
\emph on
computably dominated
\emph default
,
 if every function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 computed by 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is dominated by some computable function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
This notion is of independent interest,
 and also coincides with computability-theoretic properties that don't require talking about 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

growth rate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to discuss.
 One such property is that of 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 computing a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hyperimmune set
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is a notion that we don't have time to discuss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Curiously,
 this property is incompatible with some other lowness properties,
 such as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lowness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
 We say that 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is low if 
\begin_inset Formula $X'\equiv0'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 No degree below 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 is hyperimmune,
 except for 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Draw hyperimmune degrees in the drawing as a thing going off to the sides.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The degrees in between these two places have the property of being able to define functions that are not dominated by any computable function,
 but cannot dominate all of them.
\end_layout

